It is possible to supply the username and password as part of the git url
```
git clone https://username:password@github.com/username/repository.git
```

The problem with this is, that the password would be exposed everywhere and the repository wouldn't be private anymore. 

Some of the options I see

1) We could modify the checkout routine [1] to check if a password is part of the url and remove it in the database. 
I am not a big fan of doing this to be honest as we could leak the password when a job starts and it wouldn't be very secure.
We should probably check (in the frontend) if the url contains a password and not accept the job to protect the user.
```
function containsPassword(gitString) {
    try {
        const url = new URL(gitString);
        return !!url.password;
    } catch (e) {
        return false;
    }
}
```

2) We could add a username and password field to the submission form. This is probably fairly simple. Not to sure about allowing people to upload a ssh key. But should also not be too hard to implement. Quite some security implications there though if the db leaks somehow. Currently we assume everything to be public.

3) We could add a GitHub/ GitLab integration. This would imply that the user would authenticate with the GMT and then we could access all the repos. I think this is probably the right way to go but would be quite some engineering. 


[1] https://github.com/green-coding-berlin/green-metrics-tool/blob/2f397508a2db22402900888b21b70ed8ff01fde5/runner.py#L189

